<figure><img src="https://baty.net/img/2026/20261002-2025-Roll-055-11.webp" alt="Black and white film photo of a seagle over water"><figcaption>Gull (2025) / Leica MP. Summilux 35mm.</figcaption></figure><p><a href="https://sals.place/blog/re-comfortable-in-linux/">Sal's follow-up</a> to my post about <a href="https://baty.net/posts/2026/09/i-am-comfortable-living-in-linux-but/">ending my Linux experiment</a> includes a note about Apple Messages. I can't believe I didn't mention that. One of the biggest drawbacks for me on Linux  was not having easy access to Messages. (Nearly) everyone I correspond with uses Messages. I hate typing anything on my iPhone, so having to switch to my phone to text someone was no fun at all. And forget about sharing a funny meme or whatever. I could never get KDE Connect, LocalSend, and friends to work fast enough or consistently enough to make it worth it. It was one hurdle I was never able to clear when using Linux.</p>
